{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-96745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions has the domain ${1,2}?$\n\n- $p:{1,2}\\to R$\n- $q:R\\to [1,2)$\n- $r:[1,2)\\to {1,2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f9c3c4cd2fd5a73c1857f65733b24a07e10a77a2cb1e487450b3ac0b80843e"} {"question" "q-96745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The domain of $p$ is ${1,2},$ the set containing the two numbers $1$ and $2$ only.\n\n- The domain of $q$ is $R,$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The domain of $r$ is $[1,2),$ the interval from $1$ to $2$ (including $1$ but not including $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f9c3c4cd2fd5a73c1857f65733b24a07e10a77a2cb1e487450b3ac0b80843e"} {"question" "q-96745" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f655e9e4af250bb6f9f43018e3cfe012e6a10dbafb1483ab3ba97af93a8ddc5f"} {"question" "q-96745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/state.json" "file_sha256" "36f9c3c4cd2fd5a73c1857f65733b24a07e10a77a2cb1e487450b3ac0b80843e"} {"question" "q-96745" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-fiel